WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.519
Welcome to AeroTwin.

00:00:01.519 --> 00:00:06.583
This is the running prototype itself, captured screen by screen.

00:00:06.583 --> 00:00:15.192
I’ll show you what’s actually working—from changing engine readings, to the model evidence, to the operator tools.

00:00:15.192 --> 00:00:16.712
Let’s jump in.

00:00:16.712 --> 00:00:18.418
Start with Flightline Overview.

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:21.405
The selected virtual engine is right here.

00:00:21.405 --> 00:00:26.098
RPM, cylinder temperature, oil pressure, and anomaly score are updating together.

00:00:26.098 --> 00:00:31.218
This is a working software simulation—not a mockup—but every reading is synthetic.

00:00:31.218 --> 00:00:34.205
No real engine or aircraft is connected.

00:00:34.205 --> 00:00:34.915
Open Telemetry.

00:00:34.915 --> 00:00:39.888
Here are the live sensor cards, and below them, the trends the twin sees.

00:00:39.888 --> 00:00:45.928
Temperature, pressure, vibration, and fuel signals are tracked together so you can follow what changes, and when.

00:00:45.928 --> 00:00:47.064
Now Fault Prediction.

00:00:47.064 --> 00:00:51.611
Random Forest classifies the simulated fault; Isolation Forest checks for unusual patterns.

00:00:51.611 --> 00:00:56.536
These are separate signals: the current screen shows a Normal class at 91.

00:00:56.536 --> 00:00:59.567
2 percent confidence and a zero anomaly score.

00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:02.977
Try a simulated fault and watch the evidence change.

00:01:02.977 --> 00:01:07.523
The models were trained on synthetic data, not real engine test records.

00:01:07.523 --> 00:01:14.145
This is the actual Digital Twin inspection view: a three-dimensional reference engine alongside the selected twin’s condition.

00:01:14.145 --> 00:01:18.040
You can rotate the model and inspect its mapped components.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.324
It is a visual reference, not a sensor-equipped, physically synchronized engine.

00:01:22.324 --> 00:01:26.998
Mission Reliability turns the current simulated condition into a phase-by-phase mission picture.

00:01:26.998 --> 00:01:31.283
Explore how the score and modeled risk respond to engine health.

00:01:31.283 --> 00:01:35.956
This is a transparent prototype indicator—not a certified probability of mission success.

00:01:35.956 --> 00:01:38.419
Fleet runs three isolated virtual engine twins.

00:01:38.419 --> 00:01:42.290
Select another one to change which engine feeds the main dashboard.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:46.160
Each is a separate software simulation, not a real aircraft connection.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:51.041
On Virtual UAV, watch the illustrative route and linked engine readings side by side.

00:01:51.041 --> 00:01:56.967
Route progress follows the software simulation, and the page explicitly says it is not a navigation map.

00:01:56.967 --> 00:01:59.756
Nothing here sends commands to a real aircraft.

00:01:59.756 --> 00:02:02.813
Now ask the assistant a question about the selected engine.

00:02:02.813 --> 00:02:08.010
The public demo answers from the latest synthetic twin snapshot, so the response changes with the evidence.

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:14.124
This browser version does not connect to a hosted language model; an optional model is only configured on the backend.

00:02:14.124 --> 00:02:17.968
The Data Lab is where you can bring a CSV.

00:02:17.968 --> 00:02:24.503
It establishes a measured baseline, checks configured signal limits, and looks for persistent changes across consecutive samples.

00:02:24.503 --> 00:02:27.578
These are prototype screening rules—not validated engine limits.

00:02:27.578 --> 00:02:29.222
Finally, the SOS drill.

00:02:29.222 --> 00:02:32.509
It previews an incident brief for demonstration only.

00:02:32.509 --> 00:02:38.673
It does not detect a real crash, find a help center, or send an SOS.

00:02:38.673 --> 00:02:47.713
That is AeroTwin today: a connected, interactive software prototype for exploring the monitoring workflow—clearly synthetic, and not connected to a real aircraft.

